Fall back to the group "nogroup" when "nobody" does not exist.

Without a user directive, nginx started as root runs its workers as
NGX_USER and NGX_GROUP. configure picks the group from the build host's
/etc/group: "nobody" on the Red Hat family (where edgeweir-openresty is
built), "nogroup" on Debian and Ubuntu, which have no "nobody" group. One
edgeweir-openresty build serves both families, so the choice is made at
run time instead, as nginx built on each distribution behaves.

--- a/src/core/nginx.c
+++ b/src/core/nginx.c
@@ -1209,6 +1209,13 @@
 
         ngx_set_errno(0);
         grp = getgrnam(NGX_GROUP);
+
+        if (grp == NULL && ngx_strcmp(NGX_GROUP, "nobody") == 0) {
+            /* Debian and Ubuntu name the group of "nobody" "nogroup" */
+            ngx_set_errno(0);
+            grp = getgrnam("nogroup");
+        }
+
         if (grp == NULL) {
             ngx_log_error(NGX_LOG_EMERG, cycle->log, ngx_errno,
                           "getgrnam(\"" NGX_GROUP "\") failed");
